				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="3762904"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1520774209 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Mar 2018 13:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3735459">
									

<p>Is this well-written? Absolutely. Phenomenal writing, for sure.</p>
<p>That said, I find this hard to enjoy. I feel that this SCP (like so many others) are just trying so damn hard to seem important, to be the biggest and baddest of all SCP's, and to end the world. SCP-990 is a dream man who warns people about the end of days, SCP-1678 is an underground city built for the end of days, SCP-2399 is an enormous destroyer on Jupiter that can bring about the end of days, along with countless other SCP's entirely premised on the end of days - many even directly causing the end. At this point, the concept in-and-of-itself deserves it's own full canon <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">again</span> considering how overused it has become.</p>
<p>I understand and respect that this is the amalgamation of your works, but the fact that it led to the same place as so many other SCP's cripples my ability to enjoy it. We've heard about Lifted Veil protocol and the Foundation going public (there's even a canon for it), we've heard about how *this* new SCP is the biggest-and-baddest threat out there (many times in many ways now), we've heard about our reality falling apart at the seams with the Foundation seemingly hopeless to stop it, and we've heard the dramatic lines all essentially saying, &quot;This is it, we can't go back to being scared cavemen, either we win or shit's fucked.&quot;</p>
<p>Won't upvote for the above-stated issues, won't downvote because the writing itself is still remarkable. In the end, it all just seems to come to above-average writing with a below-average subject matter.</p>

							</div>

								
